Inteligeo, load test of 2026-11-11/12 (no advisory, no upstream fix): creating or
updating a dataset no longer resets GeoServer.

Target: GeoNode 4.3.1 as installed by this image (site-packages/geonode). Applied
with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` from site-packages (paths are geonode/...).

What happened: after Inteligeo-load-2-geoserver-reset-on-read.patch, the 100-user
round still logged "DataSource not available after calling dispose()" in one burst
(61 lines in 9 seconds) right after uploads were accepted. Two calls of
gs_catalog.reset() (POST /rest/reset, which makes GeoServer dispose the connections
of every store) were still on that path:
- sync_instance_with_geoserver, which GeoServerResourceManager.create and .update call
  for every dataset: an upload through geonode-importer reaches it at least twice
  (create, then the update of the XML metadata step), and every metadata edit once;
- clean_styles, which set_styles calls at the end of every such sync.
That these resets cause the burst is inferred from the code and the timing, not proved.

The change: sync_instance_with_geoserver keeps only the clearing of the client cache
(gs_catalog._cache.clear()); clean_styles drops the reset, since get_dataset, which it
calls next, clears the client cache itself (Inteligeo-load-2).

Who relied on the reset, read before removing it (the data loads were missed at
first and added after review):
- What both functions read (GET /rest/layers, /rest/.../featuretypes, styles) and
  write (gs_catalog.save, style deletion) is GeoServer's configuration through the REST
  API, which /rest/reset does not touch.
- A new dataset: geonode-importer creates the table with ogr2ogr and publishes the
  feature type through the REST API before the sync; GeoServer reads a new table
  without a reset, and the reset came after the publication anyway.
- The replace of a vector dataset: ogr2ogr -overwrite drops and recreates the table,
  and GeoServer keeps the old schema in its caches until a reset. Before, the reset of
  the first sync refreshed them, and the DescribeFeatureType of the second sync read
  the new attributes. Now overwrite_geoserver_resource of Inteligeo's
  importer/handlers/common/vector.py (inteligeo-geonode, same date) resets GeoServer
  once, right after ogr2ogr; this patch must not ship without that change.
- The data loads of the maintenance container (inteligeo-deploy,
  imagens_locais/manutencao/carga_de_dados): the full load rebuilds the table of an
  existing layer with an ogr2ogr dump (-overwrite, DROP and CREATE), and the full
  reload of the Brasil MAIS alerts swaps the table with DROP and CREATE; both then ask
  GeoNode to sync (/gs/updatelayers), and that sync's reset refreshed the schema.
  They now post /rest/reset themselves, once per load, right after the table is
  rebuilt (LayerManager.reset_geoserver, inteligeo-deploy, 2026-11-12); this patch must
  not ship without that change either. The load's UPSERT does not rebuild the table.
  Searched for other paths that rebuild the table of a published layer
  (ogr2ogr -overwrite, DROP TABLE) in inteligeo-deploy, inteligeo-geonode and
  inteligeo-api: the other DROP TABLEs are of staging tables or of the table of an
  import being rolled back. A table changed outside these tools (by hand, or one used
  with the load's --from-postgis) needs a reset by hand, as it would have before any
  sync.
- The replace of a raster dataset: geonode-importer deletes the coverage store through
  the REST API (which disposes that store's reader) and publishes it again.
- The legacy `overwrite` branch of the sync (geoserver_upload) changes data after the
  point where the reset was, so it never relied on it.

Not changed: the reset before the legacy GeoServer importer's append and replace
(_execute_resource_import in geoserver/manager.py), not reached by Inteligeo's
uploads; the two of cascading_delete (helpers.py), one per deletion: the one before
the search for the resource and the one that releases a coverage store's readers
before its files are deleted; geoserver/upload.py and upload/utils.py (legacy upload
and mosaics).

Files touched: geonode/geoserver/helpers.py (after Inteligeo-load-2 and -3, which
edit other functions of the same file).

Test run (2026-11-12): applied with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0 --no-backup-if-mismatch` on a
copy of site-packages/geonode taken from the 5.5.4 image (which already carries the
earlier patches of this folder), in the order the Dockerfile applies them: 1 file, 2 hunks, 0 FAILED,
0 fuzz; `python -m py_compile` clean. Not run in a pod: an upload, a metadata edit, a
style upload and a replace of a vector dataset with a changed schema have to be
repeated in the mirror, and the GeoServer log read for the dispose() message.

